   outcome still needs replication as no control group M;as involved and
   because of the occurrence of pre-treatment differences between the CO-OP
   and CTA groups on relevant measures. Follow-up data indicated that
   children who received CO-OP tended to experience greater long-term
   maintenance of their motor goals and acquired strategies: follow-up
   parent-report rated CO-OP treatment as more useful than CTA treatment.
