Organization = {Bulgarian Acad Sci; Acad Sci Czech Republ; Latvian Acad Sci; Polish Acad
   Sci; Russian Acad Sci; Serbian Acad Sci \& Arts; Slovak Acad Sci Intro;
   Natl Acad Sci Ukraine; Natl Acad Sci Armenia; Sci Counc Japan; World
   Acad Sci TWAS; European Acad Sci, Arts \& Lett; Acad Fine Arts Zagreb
   Croatia; Croatian Acad Sci \& Arts; Acad Sci Moldova; Montenegrin Acad
   Sci \& Arts; Georgian Acad Sci; Acad Fine Arts \& Design Bratislava;
   Russian Acad Arts; Turkish Acad Sci; SGEM},
Abstract = {Background: The aim of this study was to investigate the effects of oral
   health issues of children aged 6-12 living in the Danube Delta Romania,
   on their quality of life, as assessed by the children themselves as well
   as by their parents/guardians. Methods: This study had a cross-sectional
   design and was conducted in all 595 school children aged 6-12 years old
   living in the Danube Delta Biosphere Reserve, an isolated area of
   Romania. The research protocol received all the approvals on behalf of
   the ethical local authorities and each individual school. Informed
   consent from each parent or guardian was received for each child
   included in the study. All children were clinically examined based on
   the World Health Organization 1997 criteria. Dental caries, periodontal
   health, oral hygiene, orthodontic status and dental fluorosis were
   assessed. Results: The overall mean DMFT was 2.01 (range 0 13). Gingival
   bleeding was found in 32.8\% of the children and oral hygiene had
   worsened with age. Orthodontic status was found having good status (89\%
   of children presented an IOTN score of 1 or 2) and only 2\% had any
   noticeable fluorosis. The children with oral health issues present a
   significantly lower oral health related quality of life than children
   without oral health problems, assessed both by the children and their
   parents/guardians was assessed with MOHRQOL index. Conclusions: Due the
   lownumber of caries free children living in the Danube Delta Biosphere
   Reserve and the high prevalence of children with gingival bleeding and
   poor oral hygiene, the status of oral health was found poor and proved
   to affect the children's OHRQOL. Oral health related QOL can be assessed
   validly and reliable both in self-reports from children as well as by
   asking their parents/guardians about their perceptions on their
   children's oral health related QOL. Oral health education programs are
   necessary for the children living in this poor economic development
   region, with limited access to dental services.},
