Title = {Application of remote sensing for the assessment of environmental
   changes in Western Kordofan State (Sudan)},
Booktitle = {DESERTIFICATION IN THE THIRD MILLENNIUM},
Year = {2003},
Pages = {445-451},
Note = {Desertification Conference Toward Better Management of Arid and
   Semi-Arid Lands in the 21st Century, DUBAI, U ARAB EMIRATES, FEB 12-16,
   2000},
Organization = {Zayed Prize Fdn},
Abstract = {In this study, environmental changes in Western Kordofan State were
   evaluated utilizing Landsat Thematic Mapper (TM) for the years 1987 and
   1997. Augmented with ground truth, parameters assessed were vegetation,
   landuse types, settlements and drainage patterns. The TM images were
   visually interpreted and GIS (ILWIS 2.2) was used in the production of
   maps for both years. The study revealed that both the natural and human
   environments have undergone significant changes. The devastating impacts
   of the drought of the seventies and eighties have been manifested in the
   reduction of the plant cover and the appearance of barelands in the
   northern section of the area. Signs of recovery in vegetation, which
   were linked to the recovery of rainfall to normal levels, were noted in
   1997 such as the disappearance of bare patches. The cultivated areas
   have advanced by the late nineties at the expense of rangelands as an
   indirect indicator of man-made land degradation. Degradation was also
   linked with low crop productivity and the shortening of the fallow
   periods and aggravated by tree cutting and overgrazing. No changes in
   the principal zonation of the natural vegetation were noted. The number
   of settlements-has escalated in the nineties following their significant
   decrease in the eighties. Utilization of remote sensing data supported
   by fieldwork and GIS afforded a suitable means of assessments and
   delineation of different vegetation zones, settlements, drainage systems
   and landuse types. However, the small scale of Landsat images was a
   major constraint in such studies. For sustainable utilization of
   resources it is strongly recommended that the area north of 13degreesN
   should be left for animal husbandry with water and vital services
   reaching both man and animals. Small holdings could be cultivated for
   subsistence With drought resistant varieties. The indigenous attitudes
   and perceptions of the local people towards the environment must be
   considered in all policies and plans to conserve and develop the area.},
