Pages = {415-422},
Note = {International Conference on Sexual and Reproductive Health, MUMBAI,
   INDIA, 1998},
Organization = {Indian Soc Stud Reproduct \& Fertil; UNDP; UNFPA; WHO; World Bank
   Special Programme Res, Dev \& Res Training Human Reproduct},
Abstract = {The objective of the study was to assess acceptability of voluntary
   human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) testing and impact of counselling,
   thereby facilitating the identification of seropositive women early in
   pregnancy and prevention of further transmission of the disease. Pretest
   counselling was offered to 250 antenatal women individually and they
   were questioned on their knowledge, risk behaviour and perceptions. Of
   250 antenatal women, 25 were unmarried. Of the 225 married women, with
   mean age 24.1 years, 35 per cent were illiterate, 50 per cent school
   drop-outs, and 70 per cent belonged to lower strata. About 43 per cent
   (n=97) were aware of HIV, of these 67 per cent knew of its sexual and 8
   per cent its perinatal transmission. About 48 per cent knew that
   acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS) was preventable, and 23 per
   cent thought it was incurable. Only 2 per cent of the women, compared to
   25 per cent of their spouses had multiple partners. Seven per cent had a
   history of sexually transmitted diseases (STDs) themselves or their
   partner, 10 per cent had a blood transfusion, and 10 per cent had been
   tattooed. Seventeen per cent had used condoms although 30 per cent were
   aware of its role. Surprisingly, only 4 per cent had perceived that they
   were at risk of infection. Ninety per cent consented to undergo the
   testing but only 25 per cent returned for collecting the results.
   Of the 25 unmarried pregnant women, of a mean age of 17.1 years, all
   were school drop-outs and from lower strata, 12 per cent were aware of
   HIV and only one knew of its sexual transmission. They were unaware of
   the prognosis of AIDS, its preventive measures or risk of HIV infection.
   Twenty-five per cent gave a history of multiple partners,and 50 per cent
   of STDs themselves or in their partner. All consented to HIV testing.
   Seroprevalence in the sample was 2.67 per cent. Although small, the
   unmarried teenage pregnant group was particularly different. Despite
   lack of awareness women consented to testing for fear of transmission to
   the foetus but failed to return to collect the results. The reason for
   this needs to be looked into.},
