   Associates; Geol Soc London, Hydrogeol Grp; Overseas Dev Adm; UNESCO,
   Div Water Sci; WHO UNEP Global Environm Monitoring Syst; Severn Trent
   Water; Entec Europe Ltd; NIREX UK Ltd; Environm Agcy},
Abstract = {This paper presents an extreme example of a rising water table situation
   typical of that currently occurring in a number of Arabian Gulf cities
   where a fast expanding, affluent population has demanded the production
   of large quantities of desalinated seawater. This has resulted in
   estimated infiltration volumes from a combination of leaking pipes and
   sewers, septic tanks and garden watering of more than two hundred times
   the natural rainfall recharge to the pre-urbanised area. The consequence
   of such a massive overloading of the natural groundwater system is a
   predictable rise in the water table. This has already resulted in
   breakthrough of poor quality groundwater to the surface in the lower
   lying areas of the city, leaving them either permanently flooded or
   permanently damp and salty from the high evaporation rates, with
   consequent deleterious effects on roads and built structures.},
